  "query_preview": "dry weather continues across the area through at least\nearly next week courtesy of sprawling high pressure across the \nNE/SE CONUS. A shortwave passing northeast of the area will \nattempt to push a rather weak and dry backdoor cold front \ntowards the area late Thursday and Thursday night, but it more \nor less will just wash out across the area if it even makes it \nthis far south, and will have no noteworthy affect on our \nweather other than perhaps a brief increase in cloud cover. \nOtherwise, near wall-to-wall clear skies are expected into early\nnext week. Seasonable high temperatures today and tomorrow will\nwarm to ~5 degrees above normal Saturday onward w/ return \ndownsloping flow. Minimum afternoon relative humidity values \nwill continue to generally range from the mid 20s into the 30s, \nbut amid quite light surface flow, with occasional breezes of 10\nmph or greater confined to mainly the mountains. Fire danger \nshould remain relatively low overall given the progged winds, \nbut fine fuels will continue to dry out.\n\nThe overnight periods will continue to feature chilly temperatures\ngiven very efficient radiational cooling expected each night. \nThis will result in at least patchy frost potential each night,\nwith a Frost Advisory currently in effect until 9 AM this \nmorning across portions of southeast Ohio and the \ncentral/northern lowlands of West Virginia. The need for \nadditional targeted Frost Advisories for portions of the area \ntonight and going forward is anticipated, but will be correlated\nto how significantly drier air aloft mixes down each afternoon.\nLows each night through the weekend will generally range from \nthe upper 20s in the mountain valleys, while as warm as low 40s\nalong the river valleys given steam fog, with 30s anticipated \nacross the majority of the area. Locally dense fog remains \npossible each morning towards dawn in the 'typical' spots. A \nwidespread season ending freeze is not anticipated across the \nremaining active growing season in the CWA through at least mid\nto late next week.",
